WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.420
A Bash script should invoke SCP and rsync in a way that is easy to review.

2
00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:17.320
The executable, stable options, source, destination, identity, host, port, and mode should appear as clear pieces of the invocation.

3
00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:21.500
That is safer than one large command string assembled from variable data.

4
00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:23.780
Arrays help preserve structure.

5
00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:35.800
A script can keep options and operands as separate values, add supported optional arguments deliberately, and pass the final list without asking Bash to reinterpret generated shell text.

6
00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:41.500
This is especially useful for rsync, where option sets can grow as workflows mature.

7
00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:44.660
Quoting protects values that should remain literal.

8
00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:56.120
Local paths, remote paths, user names, host names, ports, and option values should not accidentally split into multiple words or become shell syntax.

9
00:00:56.220 --> 00:01:01.580
Quoting does not decide whether a value is allowed, but it preserves accepted values during invocation.

10
00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:04.660
Validation decides what the workflow supports.

11
00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:13.480
Hosts, users, ports, source paths, destination paths, and relevant option values should be checked before execution.

12
00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:21.100
A target label can map to a known host and base directory instead of allowing free-form remote destinations.

13
00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:25.220
Avoid generated command strings and unnecessary re-parsing.

14
00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:31.800
When a script stores a full transfer command in a string, reviewers must reason about another shell interpretation step.

15
00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:36.140
That makes scope, quoting, and safety harder to inspect.

16
00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:40.880
Fixed structure with separate arguments is easier to test and maintain.

17
00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:47.720
The review question is simple: can another engineer see exactly what data might move and where it might land?

18
00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:54.620
If the invocation makes that answer clear, mistakes are easier to catch before transfer automation reaches production.
